1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.370
Your model is right.

2
00:00:01.371 --> 00:00:03.482
And your users still won't bet on it.

3
00:00:03.483 --> 00:00:05.011
That half-second of doubt?

4
00:00:05.012 --> 00:00:06.661
It's the only thing I design.

5
00:00:06.662 --> 00:00:10.011
This is my process — five minutes, on the real work.

6
00:00:10.012 --> 00:00:11.127
Four products.

7
00:00:11.128 --> 00:00:12.656
An ad-trading platform.

8
00:00:12.657 --> 00:00:16.236
Two LLM products reading financial documents.

9
00:00:16.237 --> 00:00:18.761
And a learning platform for engineers.

10
00:00:18.762 --> 00:00:22.426
Every number you'll hear is the locked figure from the case page.

11
00:00:22.427 --> 00:00:27.753
Where a client is under NDA, the screens are labelled reconstructions.

12
00:00:27.754 --> 00:00:29.671
It ends with the part where I got it wrong.

13
00:00:29.672 --> 00:00:33.251
Because a process that can't show its failures isn't a process.

14
00:00:33.252 --> 00:00:34.222
It's a pitch.

15
00:00:35.126 --> 00:00:36.850
Everything starts with listening.

16
00:00:36.851 --> 00:00:41.462
And listening means being ready to hear that the brief itself is wrong.

17
00:00:41.463 --> 00:00:47.225
At PTC, the brief said: redesign the UX of five learning platforms.

18
00:00:47.226 --> 00:00:49.462
The research said something else.

19
00:00:49.463 --> 00:00:53.450
Customers didn't recognise any of our five product names.

20
00:00:53.451 --> 00:00:56.787
They already called all of it PTC University.

21
00:00:56.788 --> 00:00:58.025
And underneath that?

22
00:00:58.026 --> 00:01:02.675
The business model — perpetual licenses — was the broken interface.

23
00:01:02.676 --> 00:01:03.962
Not the navigation.

24
00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:06.325
So the listening overturned the brief.

25
00:01:06.326 --> 00:01:12.600
We retired four of five products and consolidated under the one name customers already used.

26
00:01:12.601 --> 00:01:19.626
That decision — not a visual refresh — is what every number at the end of this video traces back to.

27
00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:24.603
Structure is where listening becomes the decision that matters.

28
00:01:24.604 --> 00:01:31.968
At Talon Outdoor — the UK's largest out-of-home agency — the recommendation model was beating the traders.

29
00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:33.696
And they overrode it anyway.

30
00:01:33.697 --> 00:01:34.382
Why?

31
00:01:34.383 --> 00:01:35.840
They weren't being stubborn.

32
00:01:35.841 --> 00:01:37.360
They were being rational.

33
00:01:37.361 --> 00:01:41.318
A bare ranked list gives an expert nothing to do with their doubt.

34
00:01:41.319 --> 00:01:43.022
So the model didn't change.

35
00:01:43.023 --> 00:01:44.419
What it handed over did.

36
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:52.066
A ranked list of billboards became a full campaign plan — KP Is attached, customisable in one click.

37
00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:55.105
This is the live reconstruction on my case page.

38
00:01:55.106 --> 00:01:58.071
Watch the plan reshape when I switch the priority.

39
00:01:58.072 --> 00:01:59.296
ROI first.

40
00:01:59.297 --> 00:02:02.458
Same engine output — a completely different argument.

41
00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:04.431
One a trader can stake their name on.

42
00:02:04.432 --> 00:02:09.603
And the honest credit: the bidding model and the fifty-million-bids-an-hour infrastructure?

43
00:02:09.604 --> 00:02:10.938
Engineering's win.

44
00:02:10.939 --> 00:02:17.287
The part that wasn't automatic — experts acting on the output instead of overriding it — that part was mine.

45
00:02:18.282 --> 00:02:21.733
Proving means the failure modes ship before the feature does.

46
00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:26.498
On the private-equity screener, analysts are paid to doubt confident numbers.

47
00:02:26.499 --> 00:02:28.416
So no score travels alone.

48
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:30.879
This drawer is the proof surface.

49
00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.228
Explain this score.

50
00:02:32.229 --> 00:02:39.852
And the number opens into its sources — the board pack, the audited statements, and the one claim that couldn't be verified.

51
00:02:39.853 --> 00:02:41.028
Kept visible.

52
00:02:41.029 --> 00:02:44.802
An honest “I'm not sure” instead of a fluent guess.

53
00:02:44.803 --> 00:02:46.584
And how I prove things today?

54
00:02:46.585 --> 00:02:49.740
My prototypes are working HTML.

55
00:02:49.741 --> 00:02:51.758
The site you're watching is one of them.

56
00:02:51.759 --> 00:02:55.037
This is a live capability contract on my homepage.

57
00:02:55.038 --> 00:03:00.112
It has a job it's for, a named limit, and a visible route to a human.

58
00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:02.228
Ask it something outside its scope.

59
00:03:02.229 --> 00:03:03.985
It declines — by design.

60
00:03:03.986 --> 00:03:06.696
A user's trust doesn't erode slowly.

61
00:03:06.697 --> 00:03:09.765
It dies in a single confident wrong answer.

62
00:03:09.766 --> 00:03:12.414
So the boundary ships in the interface.

63
00:03:12.415 --> 00:03:13.813
Not in the fine print.

64
00:03:14.726 --> 00:03:16.403
Now the part that didn't work.

65
00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:20.911
On PTC's accessibility pass, I did what felt thorough.

66
00:03:20.912 --> 00:03:24.714
Every element loaded with ARIA labels.

67
00:03:24.715 --> 00:03:27.042
Long, careful, exhaustive.

68
00:03:27.043 --> 00:03:32.613
Then I tested with real visually-impaired users, and watched it fail in front of me.

69
00:03:32.614 --> 00:03:34.901
They don't listen through sentences.

70
00:03:34.902 --> 00:03:38.198
They skim — by heading and landmark, fast.

71
00:03:38.199 --> 00:03:43.212
My thorough labels were slowing down exactly the people I built them for.

72
00:03:43.213 --> 00:03:49.050
So I switched my monitor off for a week and navigated the whole system by screen reader alone.

73
00:03:49.051 --> 00:03:52.281
Then I re-did the front-end with what that week taught me.

74
00:03:52.282 --> 00:03:56.430
The second version worked because the first one had failed where I could see it.

75
00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.252
Landing is measured, with baselines.

76
00:04:00.253 --> 00:04:03.463
Campaign planning: two weeks to three hours.

77
00:04:03.464 --> 00:04:08.279
Deal screening: sixty percent faster, measured before and after rollout.

78
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.955
A million dollars a year off PTC's print budget.

79
00:04:11.956 --> 00:04:17.593
And subscriptions from zero to sixty-four percent of new bookings — in five quarters.

80
00:04:17.594 --> 00:04:22.446
Every one of these is on my homepage with its measurement basis one click deep.

81
00:04:22.447 --> 00:04:24.088
And the numbers that aren't mine?

82
00:04:24.089 --> 00:04:26.135
They say so, on the card.

83
00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:27.752
Listen.

84
00:04:27.753 --> 00:04:28.731
Structure.

85
00:04:28.732 --> 00:04:29.543
Prove.

86
00:04:29.544 --> 00:04:30.406
Land.

87
00:04:30.407 --> 00:04:33.911
Then the loop restarts on what people actually did with it.

88
00:04:33.912 --> 00:04:37.623
I once argued hard for a three-option recommendation card.

89
00:04:37.624 --> 00:04:41.579
The live AB test made the single-option version permanent.

90
00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.847
The ship teaches what the mock can't.

91
00:04:43.848 --> 00:04:44.917
That's the job.

92
00:04:44.918 --> 00:04:47.919
I'm looking for one seat where this is the whole job.

93
00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.157
Not a phase of it.
